{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" Everything the process writes goes into that writable layer. When the container stops — crash, OOM kill, a `SIGTERM` it handled badly — the kubelet restarts it, and the restarted container gets a"}},"session_id":"49a7274b-da3a-49bf-afc0-fa4977a85c9d","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"46090f3d-7eab-4f47-84a1-c5039862294d"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" clean state assembled fresh from the image [source: k8s-docs-volumes-2026-08-23]. The writable layer is discarded. That is rung one, and the boundary that crosses it is **a container restart**.\n\nNow attach a volume. A volume declared in"}},"session_id":"49a7274b-da3a-49bf-afc0-fa4977a85c9d","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"ff12fbd3-96ed-4c92-ad70-b57cffb48a8c"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" the PodSpec and mounted into a container is not part of the container's filesystem stack; it is mounted into it. When the container restarts, the volume is still there, because the volume belongs to the Pod, not to the container. The documentation states this as a flat rule: *for"}},"session_id":"49a7274b-da3a-49bf-afc0-fa4977a85c9d","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"871ea4be-2135-4c9c-a5ca-55a2f8f242e0"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" any kind of volume in a given Pod, data is preserved across container restarts* [source: k8s-docs-volumes-2026-08-23]. That is rung two. It survives the boundary that killed rung one.\n\nBut rung two has a boundary of its own. **When a"}},"session_id":"49a7274b-da3a-49bf-afc0-fa4977a85c9d","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"9dd20f4b-49c2-4382-be98-c424247cf979"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" Pod ceases to exist, Kubernetes destroys ephemeral volumes** [source: k8s-docs-volumes-2026-08-23]. Not when the Pod is unhealthy, not when a container inside it dies: when"}},"session_id":"49a7274b-da3a-49bf-afc0-fa4977a85c9d","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"989c3f6a-8cb6-450d-a4fd-242dc8adbd27"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" the Pod object itself is gone. Delete the Pod, and its `emptyDir` goes with it.\n\nRung three is what is left when you ask for storage whose lifetime is not tied to any Pod at all. **Kubernetes does not destroy persistent volumes"}},"session_id":"49a7274b-da3a-49bf-afc0-fa4977a85c9d","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"a4422a82-7acf-4de8-9d33-9ea65ae8611f"}
